      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "In the past singular, how many ending sets do -er and -ir verbs need between them? (One — comí and viví take the same endings.)"
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The plural keeps that bargain."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: one ending, two families",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "comieron, vivieron — they ate, they lived."
            },
            {
              "kind": "table",
              "headers": [
                "",
                "present",
                "past"
              ],
              "columns": 3,
              "rowCount": 2,
              "utterances": [
                "comer. present: comen. past: comieron.",
                "vivir. present: viven. past: vivieron."
              ]
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The past ending is -ieron for both families, exactly as -í served both in the singular. And we follows the same shape:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "comimos, vivimos."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Now the interesting part, and it is not the one you would guess."
            },
            {
              "kind": "table",
              "headers": [
                "",
                "present",
                "past"
              ],
              "columns": 3,
              "rowCount": 3,
              "utterances": [
                "hablar. present: hablamos. past: hablamos — the same.",
                "comer. present: comemos. past: comimos — different.",
                "vivir. present: vivimos. past: vivimos — the same."
              ]
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Two families out of three collapse. Vivimos is we live and we lived, exactly as hablamos is both. Only -er keeps the two apart — and only because its past borrowed -imos from the -ir family and so moved away from its own present -emos."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You met comemos and vivimos together as the first place -er and -ir stop agreeing. This is the second place, and it goes the other way."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "comieron, vivieron",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *comieron · vivieron*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
